;;; A written fact of `eq` at one argument is stored — M1f **W61**, register
;;; row `W61/fact-eq-arity` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), filed by S1f.5.9
;;; (T1f.5.9.1, 2026-11-09), `open` and owned by S1f.5.9, found beside W60.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)`, and a fact of `eq` written with one argument, `(eq a)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `eq` is the relation of equality, between exactly two values, whose facts
;;; are computed and never stored (M1f S1f.5.7, Q-M1e2.26), and a written
;;; fact of it is checked as a conclusion with no premise (Q-M1f.33). One
;;; argument is no fact of equality, true or false, and the grammar refuses
;;; `(eq ?x)` as a premise and as a conclusion: the program is refused at
;;; load, one line naming the fact, and every command that loads it exits 1
;;; (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loads and stores `(eq a)` as an ordinary fact (`ein saturate
;;; FILE --dump`), the check of a written `eq` fact reading two arguments
;;; only; `eq` holds no candidate, and R is two states, ∅ and `{(p z)}`, exit
;;; 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at `ee3da657`, release and
;;; debug alike).

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a p")
(p a)
(hit z)
(eq a)
(query :goal (p ?x))
